   - **xcodeproj path-doubling bug** (found twice, same pattern): Once when adding `GoogleService-Info.plist` to the iOS Xcode project via the `xcodeproj` Ruby gem — `runner_group.new_file("Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist")` doubled the path since the group's own path already resolved to `Runner/`, causing `Runner/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist` and a build failure "input file cannot be found". Fixed by removing the bad reference and re-adding with just `new_file("GoogleService-Info.plist")` (relative to the already-Runner-rooted group). A very similar doubled-prefix mistake also occurred manually typing `group.` twice into the Apple Developer Portal's "Identifier" field for App Groups (`group.group.de.begod.schwarmwacht`) — fixed via `form_input` tool setting the value directly rather than keyboard typing (which kept appending instead of replacing).
   - **iOS provisioning profile mismatch**: First fresh device build after adding new Apple Portal capabilities failed with "Provisioning profile ... doesn't match the entitlements file's value for the keychain-access-groups entitlement" for both App IDs. Root cause (found via investigation, not initially assumed): `keychain-access-groups` entitlement value was a bare `group.de.begod.schwarmwacht`, but Apple requires the Team ID prefix (`$(AppIdentifierPrefix)group.de.begod.schwarmwacht`) for this specific entitlement key (unlike `application-groups`, which does not need it). Fixed in both `Runner.entitlements` and `NotificationServiceExtension.entitlements`; committed as `ca393d39f`. Rebuild succeeded and app installed/launched on the physical iPhone, verified via `devicectl device info apps`.
   - **Missing `aps-environment` entitlement**: Discovered while setting up Apple Portal capabilities that `Runner.entitlements` never had `aps-environment` set at all — without it Xcode cannot sign for real push regardless of any other configuration. Added `<key>aps-environment</key><string>development</string>` (matches the profile/debug signing path this project uses); committed as `e462e2545`.
   - **`ref.read()` inside `dispose()` crash** (most recent, in device_list_screen.dart): `_DeviceSearchDialogState.dispose()` called `ref.read(deviceDiscoveryProvider).stopScanning()` directly, causing `StateError: Cannot use "ref" after the widget was disposed` from `ConsumerStatefulElement._assertNotDisposed`. This surfaced when running the first widget test (`flutter test test/device_list_screen_test.dart`), which failed both test cases with this exception during teardown. Fixed by capturing the service instance in `initState()` as `late final DeviceDiscoveryService _discovery` and reusing that stored field in `dispose()` instead of re-reading `ref`. **This fix has been applied but not yet re-verified with a passing test run** (the verification command was interrupted by the summary request — see Current Work below).
